Task 1: Discover Managed Clusters Using the RHACM API
Subtask 1.1: Authenticate and Verify API Connectivity
Log in to the hub cluster (if not already logged in) and set environment variables
# Confirm current login context (hub cluster)
oc whoami
oc whoami --show-server

# Set the OpenShift/Kubernetes API endpoint for the hub cluster
export OCP_API_URL=$(oc whoami --show-server)

# Get the authentication token for API calls
export TOKEN=$(oc whoami -t)

# Verify the token was retrieved (only prints a short prefix, not the full secret)
echo "Token acquired: ${TOKEN:0:15}..."
Expected result: oc whoami returns a username, OCP_API_URL is set to an https://api...:6443 style URL, and the token prefix is printed.

Test connectivity to the Kubernetes API and the RHACM ManagedCluster API
# Basic API connectivity check
curl -sk -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  "$OCP_API_URL/api/v1/namespaces" | jq -r '.items[].metadata.name' | head -n 5

# Confirm the RHACM managedclusters API group is available
curl -sk -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  "$OCP_API_URL/apis/cluster.open-cluster-management.io/v1/managedclusters" | jq '.kind'
Expected result: the first command lists at least a few namespace names (for example default, kube-system, open-cluster-management); the second command prints "ManagedClusterList", confirming the RHACM API group is reachable and authenticated.

Subtask 1.2: List Managed Clusters and Their Status
Create a reusable script that discovers all managed clusters currently known to the hub, rather than hardcoding names. Every later task in this lab reuses this discovery logic so the cluster list always matches your real environment.

cat > list_clusters.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
set -euo pipefail

: "${TOKEN:?TOKEN environment variable is not set. Run: export TOKEN=$(oc whoami -t)}"
: "${OCP_API_URL:?OCP_API_URL environment variable is not set. Run: export OCP_API_URL=$(oc whoami --show-server)}"

# Function to list managed clusters and their availability status
list_managed_clusters() {
    echo "=== Managed Clusters ==="

    local response
    response=$(curl -sk -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
        "$OCP_API_URL/apis/cluster.open-cluster-management.io/v1/managedclusters")

    local count
    count=$(echo "$response" | jq '.items | length')

    if [ "$count" -eq 0 ]; then
        echo "No managed clusters found."
        return 1
    fi

    echo "$response" | jq -r '.items[] |
        "\(.metadata.name) - Available: " +
        ((.status.conditions[]? | select(.type=="ManagedClusterConditionAvailable") | .status) // "Unknown")'

    echo ""
    echo "Total managed clusters: $count"
}

# Function to print details for a single cluster
get_cluster_details() {
    local cluster_name=$1

    echo "=== Cluster Details: $cluster_name ==="

    curl -sk -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
        "$OCP_API_URL/apis/cluster.open-cluster-management.io/v1/managedclusters/$cluster_name" | \
        jq '{
            name: .metadata.name,
            labels: .metadata.labels,
            availability: (.status.conditions[]? | select(.type=="ManagedClusterConditionAvailable") | .status),
            kubernetesVersion: .status.version.kubernetes
        }'
}

# Main execution
list_managed_clusters

echo ""
echo "=== Detailed Cluster Information ==="
cluster_names=$(curl -sk -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
    "$OCP_API_URL/apis/cluster.open-cluster-management.io/v1/managedclusters" | \
    jq -r '.items[].metadata.name')

for cluster in $cluster_names; do
    get_cluster_details "$cluster"
    echo ""
done
EOF

chmod +x list_clusters.sh
./list_clusters.sh
Deliverable: Run ./list_clusters.sh and capture its output. Expected result: a summary section listing each managed cluster with Available: True, followed by a total count, and then a detailed JSON block per cluster showing name, labels, availability, and kubernetesVersion.

Subtask 1.3: Save the Discovered Cluster List for Reuse
To keep later automation consistent with your actual environment, persist the discovered cluster names to a file that subsequent scripts will read.

# Discover cluster names dynamically and save them for reuse in Task 2
curl -sk -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
    "$OCP_API_URL/apis/cluster.open-cluster-management.io/v1/managedclusters" | \
    jq -r '.items[].metadata.name' > discovered_clusters.txt

echo "Discovered clusters saved to discovered_clusters.txt:"
cat discovered_clusters.txt
Expected result: discovered_clusters.txt contains one managed cluster name per line, matching the names shown in Subtask 1.2. If this file is empty, resolve cluster import/availability issues before continuing, since Task 2 depends on it.

Task 2: Automate Policy Creation and Compliance Checking
Subtask 2.1: Build a Configuration-Driven Policy Generator
Rather than hardcoding cluster names inside a policy template, this script reads the cluster list from discovered_clusters.txt (created in Task 1) and generates a JSON configuration file that reflects your actual lab environment.

cat > generate_config.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
set -euo pipefail

CLUSTERS_FILE="discovered_clusters.txt"
CONFIG_FILE="automation_config.json"

if [ ! -s "$CLUSTERS_FILE" ]; then
    echo "ERROR: $CLUSTERS_FILE not found or empty. Run list_clusters.sh / Task 1 Subtask 1.3 first." >&2
    exit 1
fi

# Build a JSON array of cluster names from the discovered clusters file
cluster_json=$(jq -R -s -c 'split("\n") | map(select(length > 0))' "$CLUSTERS_FILE")

# Generate automation_config.json using the ACTUAL discovered cluster names
jq -n \
  --argjson clusters "$cluster_json" \
  '{
    clusters: $clusters,
    policies: [
      {
        name: "security-baseline",
        enforcement: "inform"
      },
      {
        name: "resource-baseline",
        enforcement: "inform"
      }
    ]
  }' > "$CONFIG_FILE"

echo "Configuration file created: $CONFIG_FILE"
cat "$CONFIG_FILE"
EOF

chmod +x generate_config.sh
./generate_config.sh
Deliverable: automation_config.json exists and its clusters array contains the same cluster names as discovered_clusters.txt (not placeholder names like cluster1/cluster2/cluster3). Expected result: the printed JSON shows a clusters array matching your real environment and a policies array with two entries.

Subtask 2.2: Create and Apply a Security Policy Across Discovered Clusters
cat > create_policy.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
set -euo pipefail

CONFIG_FILE="automation_config.json"

: "${CONFIG_FILE:?}"
if [ ! -f "$CONFIG_FILE" ]; then
    echo "ERROR: $CONFIG_FILE not found. Run generate_config.sh first." >&2
    exit 1
fi

create_security_policy() {
    local policy_name=$1
    local enforcement=$2

    # Build a PlacementRule cluster-name match list from the config file
    local values_yaml
    values_yaml=$(jq -r '.clusters[] | "    - " + .' "$CONFIG_FILE")

    cat > "policy-${policy_name}.yaml" << EOFPOLICY
apiVersion: policy.open-cluster-management.io/v1
kind: Policy
metadata:
  name: ${policy_name}
  namespace: default
  annotations:
    policy.open-cluster-management.io/standards: NIST-CSF
    policy.open-cluster-management.io/categories: PR.AC Identity Management Authentication and Access Control
    policy.open-cluster-management.io/controls: PR.AC-4 Access Control
spec:
  remediationAction: ${enforcement}
  disabled: false
  policy-templates:
    - objectDefinition:
        apiVersion: policy.open-cluster-management.io/v1
        kind: ConfigurationPolicy
        metadata:
          name: ${policy_name}-config
        spec:
          remediationAction: ${enforcement}
          severity: medium
          namespaceSelector:
            exclude:
              - kube-*
            include:
              - default
          object-templates:
            - complianceType: musthave
              objectDefinition:
                apiVersion: v1
                kind: NetworkPolicy
                metadata:
                  name: deny-all-ingress
                  namespace: default
                spec:
                  podSelector: {}
                  policyTypes:
                    - Ingress
---
apiVersion: policy.open-cluster-management.io/v1
kind: PlacementBinding
metadata:
  name: binding-${policy_name}
  namespace: default
placementRef:
  name: placement-${policy_name}
  kind: PlacementRule
  apiGroup: apps.open-cluster-management.io
subjects:
  - name: ${policy_name}
    kind: Policy
    apiGroup: policy.open-cluster-management.io
---
apiVersion: apps.open-cluster-management.io/v1
kind: PlacementRule
metadata:
  name: placement-${policy_name}
  namespace: default
spec:
  clusterConditions:
    - status: "True"
      type: ManagedClusterConditionAvailable
  clusterSelector:
    matchExpressions:
      - key: name
        operator: In
        values:
$values_yaml
EOFPOLICY

    oc apply -f "policy-${policy_name}.yaml"
}

# Read each policy definition from the config and create it
jq -c '.policies[]' "$CONFIG_FILE" | while read -r policy; do
    name=$(echo "$policy" | jq -r '.name')
    enforcement=$(echo "$policy" | jq -r '.enforcement')
    echo "Creating policy: $name (enforcement: $enforcement)"
    create_security_policy "$name" "$enforcement"
done

echo ""
echo "=== Policies currently defined on the hub ==="
oc get policies -n default
EOF

chmod +x create_policy.sh
./create_policy.sh
Deliverable: oc get policies -n default lists security-baseline and resource-baseline. Expected result: both policies show remediationAction as inform in the oc get policies -n default -o wide output, and no oc apply errors were printed.

Subtask 2.3: Check Policy Compliance and Wrap It in a Reusable Function
cat > check_compliance.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
set -euo pipefail

CONFIG_FILE="automation_config.json"

: "${CONFIG_FILE:?}"
if [ ! -f "$CONFIG_FILE" ]; then
    echo "ERROR: $CONFIG_FILE not found. Run generate_config.sh and create_policy.sh first." >&2
    exit 1
fi

check_policy_compliance() {
    local policy_name=$1

    echo "=== Compliance for policy: $policy_name ==="

    local overall
    overall=$(oc get policy "$policy_name" -n default -o json 2>/dev/null | jq -r '.status.compliant // "Pending"')
    echo "Overall status: $overall"

    echo "Per-cluster status:"
    oc get policy "$policy_name" -n default -o json 2>/dev/null | \
        jq -r '.status.status[]? | "  \(.clustername): \(.compliant)"'

    echo ""
}

# Give the policy controller a moment to evaluate compliance
echo "Waiting for policy evaluation..."
sleep 15

jq -r '.policies[].name' "$CONFIG_FILE" | while read -r policy_name; do
    check_policy_compliance "$policy_name"
done
EOF

chmod +x check_compliance.sh
./check_compliance.sh
Deliverable: For each policy in automation_config.json, the script prints an overall status (Compliant, NonCompliant, or Pending) and a per-cluster breakdown. Expected result: within a few minutes of applying the policies, each managed cluster listed in automation_config.json shows a compliance value of Compliant or NonCompliant (not indefinitely Pending); Pending immediately after creation is normal and should resolve on a re-run.

