Troubleshooting
Issue 1: list_clusters.sh prints "No managed clusters found" or an empty list Symptom: discovered_clusters.txt ends up empty, which then causes generate_config.sh to fail. Diagnostic hint: run oc get managedclusters directly on the hub to confirm at least one cluster is imported and its AVAILABLE column shows True; also confirm TOKEN and OCP_API_URL are set in your current shell session (echo $OCP_API_URL).

Issue 2: Policy compliance stays Pending indefinitely in check_compliance.sh Symptom: repeated runs of check_compliance.sh never show Compliant or NonCompliant. Diagnostic hint: check that the PlacementRule actually selected clusters with oc get placementrule placement-<policy_name> -n default -o yaml and look at status.decisions; an empty decisions list means the clusterSelector values did not match any real managed cluster names, which usually means automation_config.json is stale â rerun generate_config.sh.

Cleanup
Remove the resources created during this lab so the hub cluster is left in its original state:

# Delete policies, placement bindings, and placement rules created in Task 2
jq -r '.policies[].name' automation_config.json | while read -r policy_name; do
    oc delete policy "$policy_name" -n default --ignore-not-found
    oc delete placementbinding "binding-$policy_name" -n default --ignore-not-found
    oc delete placementrule "placement-$policy_name" -n default --ignore-not-found
done

# Remove locally generated files
rm -f list_clusters.sh generate_config.sh create_policy.sh check_compliance.sh \
      discovered_clusters.txt automation_config.json policy-*.yaml

echo "Cleanup complete."
Expected result: oc get policies -n default no longer lists security-baseline or resource-baseline, and the working directory contains none of the generated scripts or YAML files.

Conclusion
In this lab, you worked directly with the RHACM API to authenticate against a hub cluster, dynamically discover managed clusters, and persist that discovery into a reusable configuration file rather than relying on hardcoded cluster names. You then used that configuration to programmatically generate and apply Policy, PlacementRule, and PlacementBinding resources across every discovered cluster, and built a compliance-checking script to verify the results. Through this process you practiced a config-driven automation pattern that keeps scripts consistent with the real state of a multicluster environment, which is a foundational technique for building safe, repeatable RHACM automation in production. Finally, you validated your results with concrete oc get checks and cleaned up all created resources, leaving the hub cluster in its original state.
